meta {
  name: 12. Delete DataSet (triggers DELETE saga)
  type: http
  seq: 20
}

delete {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/datasets/:id
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  id: {{pgDataSetId}}
}

tests {
  test("DataSet delete accepted or already gone", function() {
    const status = res.getStatus();
    // 204=DELETE saga triggered; 404 if a previous run already removed the dataset.
    expect(status === 204 || status === 404).to.be.true;
  });
}

docs {
  **PostGIS saga step 12** — Delete the dataset, triggering the DATASET_DELETE saga.

  After the unrelease (step 11/11a) the dataset is `READY` with its PostGIS sink still provisioned. Deleting a provisioned dataset now runs the DATASET_DELETE saga, which tears down the full infrastructure — including the data-bearing PostGIS table — and the DataSet entity (with its sink and pipeline via cascade) is removed once the saga completes.

  Because the saga owns the teardown, the sink and pipeline no longer need to be deleted by hand; the later cleanup steps only remain as safety nets for a never-provisioned or partially built run.

  **Precondition:** `pgDataSetId` is set; dataset is provisioned and in `READY` (unrelease saga complete).

  **Expected Response:** 204 No Content (DELETE saga triggered). 404 if a previous run already deleted it.

  **System Impact:**

  | Layer | Effect |
  |---|---|
  | Backend | DELETE saga triggered; DataSet + sink + pipeline removed after completion |
  | Config Adapter | Full teardown: NiFi flow, APISIX route/upstream, PostGIS table de-provisioned |
  | External System | PostGIS sink table dropped |
}
